WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.600
Welcome to this English Online&nbsp;
business English series!

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:07.600
We’ll be equipping you with skills&nbsp;
that you can use to navigate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:10.240
the international English speaking workspace.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:16.800
Let’s get started. We’re going to 
start with a juicy topic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:23.760
One that a lot of people like talking&nbsp;
about but don’t really like acting upon.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:24.960
Conflict Management.

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:26.930
Now there’s a buzzword if
there ever was one.

00:00:26.930 --> 00:00:30.240
So, conflict management 101.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:34.880
Number 1: Assume positive intentions

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:41.280
Okay. This is how we often approach a&nbsp;
conversation in an attempt to manage conflict.

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:46.240
They’re going to try and manipulate&nbsp;
you, but DO NOT STAND DOWN!

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:50.000
It’s like when we’re entering a conversation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:53.840
that is attempting to manage&nbsp;
conflict, we’re going to war.

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:59.760
Remember, you want to resolve an issue, 
so&nbsp;that you and your team can move forward.

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:06.240
Start by assuming that your colleague’s&nbsp;
intentions are positive. That they’re actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:12.240
interested in the well being of the team. 
The&nbsp;truth is, they’re not looking to humiliate you.&nbsp;

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:16.240
How you think about someone changes&nbsp;
how you interact with that person.&nbsp;

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:18.800
This is an example of when
we assume the worst.

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:22.000
To be honest with you, I really&nbsp;
don’t think it was a good idea.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:25.200
You’re always looking for what
we didn’t do well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:28.400
Quite honestly it stifles&nbsp;
our creativity as a team.

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:30.720
Versus assuming the best

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:34.080
To be honest with you, I really&nbsp;
don’t think it was a good idea.&nbsp;

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:40.880
Okay, You obviously have your reasons for thinking&nbsp;that - why don’t you think it was a good idea?

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:43.280
Assume that your colleagues&nbsp;
have positive intentions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:45.520
It really will help you manage conflict.

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:48.880
Number 2: Listen and be open to change. &nbsp;

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:54.160
A conversation is two ways. If you’re the only&nbsp;person speaking, 
that’s called a monologue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:59.120
not a conversation. In a conversation&nbsp;
you need to be speaking and listening.

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:03.920
The problem is, a lot of us think we’re listening
when actually, all we’re doing is waiting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:10.160
for our chance to respond. I just thought 
you were a little bit inconsiderate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:15.680
when she asked you what you thought of the&nbsp;presentation
and you were really critical.

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:21.920
Inconsiderate? How is that inconsiderate?&nbsp;
I didn’t want her to get up in front of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:27.840
our directors with a presentation that was&nbsp;
going to make sure we don’t get the funding.

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:32.080
these kinds of things just so that we&nbsp;
can grow together as a team and really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:34.640
get to where we need to be. 
Person 2: Mm yes, sorry, just to…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:41.600
go back to when you said that I was inconsiderate, 
I just need to say that I really don’t agree.

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:48.480
Remaining silent as we wait for our chance to&nbsp;respond
is not the same thing as listening.

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:52.800
Let me ask you a question. How often&nbsp;
do you prepare your counter attack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:58.880
whilst pretending to listen? Put your thoughts aside for the moment and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:01.713
truly listen when your colleague is speaking.

00:03:02.362 --> 00:03:04.985
I just thought you were a little bit&nbsp;inconsiderate. &nbsp;

00:03:04.985 --> 00:03:09.200
She’s been working on it for a&nbsp;
really long time and I really think that just with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:14.320
a little bit more encouragement and maybe just&nbsp;
looking out for the positives that it could really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:17.207
kind of boost her and bring out the best in her.

00:03:17.207 --> 00:03:21.117
Growth and development are only possible if&nbsp;you allow their words

00:03:21.377 --> 00:03:24.648
to change you, and your attitude and your responses.

